#!/usr/bin/env node

// Derives the _AREA_ANCHORS layer: anchor points per area feature of the
// ship-narrow class set, at which the style draws the feature's centred area
// symbol (the PILBOP02 diamond, the CTNARE51 caution mark and their siblings)
// instead of drawing it once per polygon per tile. Three treatments
// (2026-08-21 ruling): a FILLED class gets ONE point per (feature, interval)
// with its charted SCAMIN; a no-fill class in GRID_REPEAT_CLASSES gets a
// ground-distance GRID of points with SCAMIN dropped, so the symbol that is
// the only sign the area exists is never a screenful away; and a no-fill
// class in SINGLE_ANCHOR_CLASSES keeps the one point and the charted SCAMIN
// -- the ruling judged a repeated mark unhelpful there.
//
// Usage:
//   bin/generate-area-anchors <output.geojson> \
//     --class CTNARE:CTNARE.geojson [--class PILBOP:PILBOP.geojson ...] \
//     [--area-evidence area_evidence_all.geojson] [--dsnm US5OR2JC] \
//     [--cell-floor <N>]
//
//   bin/generate-area-anchors --list-classes
//     prints the participating classes, one per line -- the same one-source
//     contract as bin/generate-area-edges, pinning bin/s57-to-tiles' loop and
//     the tests to this file's list.
//
// Why this layer exists:
//
//   tippecanoe writes each polygon CLIPPED into every tile it touches, and
//   MapLibre places one centred symbol per fragment whose pole of
//   inaccessibility lands inside its own tile -- so an area straddling a tile
//   edge draws its symbol twice, a corner up to four times, and a multi-part
//   area once more per part. Measured over central Puget at z12: 22% of these
//   layers' polygon features duplicate (RESARE 77/223, TSSLPT 71/120, CBLARE
//   55/156, CTNARE 47/120), and the Port Angeles pilot boarding area draws
//   two diamonds ~63 screen px apart. One POINT per feature lands in exactly
//   one tile at any zoom, which is what makes the symbol appear once.
//
//   This is bin/generate-restriction-anchors MINUS the water-side LNDARE
//   scanline pass -- deliberately a plain-centroid sibling rather than a
//   parameterisation: restriction polygons are drawn to their legal boundary
//   and genuinely span land, where these classes are ship-routing and caution
//   areas drawn to the water they regulate, so the natural label point --
//   bin/generate-labels' placement, the other close precedent -- is already
//   the right one.
//
// GROUPING is by (CLASS, LNAM, interval), for bin/generate-restriction-
// anchors' reasons: LNAM is S-57's feature identifier, so the group is the
// FEATURE rather than a contiguity guess and two abutting but distinct areas
// keep their own symbols; a feature with no LNAM is its own group; and the
// interval keeps the copy ladder's overlapping copies of one feature from
// merging into a single anchor stamped for no interval at all.
//
// LNAM IS NOT ENOUGH, in either direction -- it is issued per cell, so one area
// split by a chart border is several features with several LNAMs and drew a
// stack of symbols per cell it reaches, and one cell holds several LNAMs of one
// area just as readily. --area-evidence, --dsnm and --cell-floor turn on the
// ELECTION that removes both: the contiguous same-class, same-content
// component, WITHIN a cell as much as across a seam, elects the smallest DSNM
// in it and, inside that cell, the smallest group id standing on it. Every
// group is registered first and asked for its verdict second, since the
// within-cell half cannot be decided until every group has been seen.
//
// The one surviving anchor stands for the whole physical area, so it is both
// MEASURED as the whole component (AREA) and PLACED over it -- the largest
// fragment is chosen among this cell's own clipped parts AND the component's
// polygons, because the elected cell is frequently the one holding a corner
// offcut a few square metres across, where the symbol would sit at the extreme
// edge of the area. The property BAG stays this cell's (see below): only the
// placement polygon may be a neighbour's. See bin/quilt-election.mjs; without
// those inputs every group keeps its own anchor.
//
// ORIENT-CARRYING features of ORIENT_LEG_CLASSES (RCTLPT) are skipped here:
// they are directed legs and bin/generate-tss-anchors stitches them back into
// lanes. Their undirected siblings, which draw the plain centred mark, stay.
//
// Each output feature carries EVERY non-underscore property of this cell's
// LARGEST fragment -- its own, never a neighbour's, whatever part the election
// chose to place the point in: the retargeted layers' filters branch on CATPIL,
// CATBRG, WATLEV, CONRAD, CONVIS and the rest of each class's own attributes,
// and an anchor missing one falls into the wrong branch -- so the whole bag
// rides along rather than a curated list. ONE deliberate exception:
// SCAMIN/SCAMAX are dropped for GRID_REPEAT_CLASSES (see the bag copy below
// -- the interval floor owns scale gating for them). Underscore properties are
// the pipeline's own; of those, the copy ladder's _QZMIN/_QZMAX/_QFALL are
// re-added deliberately, converted to per-feature tippecanoe zoom bounds later
// by bin/stamp-quilt-zooms. On top of the bag:
//
//   CLASS    the S-57 class the feature came from, stamped over any source
//            property of that name
//   AREA     the summed area in square degrees, longitude scaled by
//            cos(latitude) (bin/quilt-anchors.mjs featureArea), computed here
//            so the style can reuse the restriction anchors' screen-size
//            density floor. The whole ELECTED COMPONENT's area where an
//            election ran (its cells' unclipped polygons, this one's
//            included), so the floor measures the feature rather than this
//            cell's share of it; the group's own summed area where the group
//            stands on no component

import { writeFileSync } from "fs";

import {
  ANCHOR_TIPPECANOE,
  ORIENT_LEG_CLASSES,
  featureArea,
  hasOrient,
  pointOnSurface,
  ringArea,
} from "./quilt-anchors.mjs";
import { contentOf } from "./quilt-content.mjs";
import { latticeNodesContained } from "./quilt-edges.mjs";
import { anchorElection } from "./quilt-election.mjs";
import {
  polygons,
  quiltKey,
  quiltRange,
  readFeatures,
} from "./quilt-geojson.mjs";

// The class set, in two shipped rounds.
//
// The SHIP-NARROW TEN came first, because that is where the measured, visible
// duplication was and it kept the property-bag question small: CTNARE and
// PILBOP draw an unconditional centred symbol and were never anchored at all;
// the other eight are in the restriction-anchor class set, but only their
// CS(RESTRN01) cascade symbols are retargeted there and their OWN symbol
// (ESSARE01, CBLARE51, TSLDEF51, ITZARE51, PRCARE51, TSSCRS51, CTNARE51,
// TSSRON51) still drew per fragment.
//
// The WIDENING ROUND then took the deferred own-symbol list: the remaining
// area classes whose unconditional centred symbol duplicates the same way,
// each with the same one-anchor-per-(CLASS, LNAM, interval) contract. (The
// TS_*/T_* tidal and timing carriers also duplicate but stay with their
// layers.)
//
// STILL OUT, deliberately, each with its reason:
//
//   ######   the QUESMRK1 unknown-object carrier is a style-side pseudo class
//            and never names a tile source-layer, so there is no per-class
//            export to anchor.
//   ACHARE   excluded by user ruling -- anchorages are fine as drawn, and
//            widening past that needs a new ruling, not a wider loop.
//   OBSTRN   a HAZARD whose centred symbols are conditional symbology, not a
//            plain per-class mark: the isolated-danger core layers precede the
//            anchor swap in the facade's settings chain (they are forced
//            visible, so an anchor copy would DOUBLE them, never replace
//            them), and its CS(OBSTRN07) branches read the pipeline's
//            _DEPARE_DRVAL1_MAX/_MINPOS columns, which the anchor property
//            bag strips as underscore-prefixed. Deduplicating it belongs with
//            the hazard family's own design round.
//
// Widening is deliberate: add the class HERE and to the bin/s57-to-tiles step
// together (--list-classes is how the tests pin the two against each other).
const AREA_ANCHOR_CLASSES = [
  // The ship-narrow ten.
  "CTNARE",
  "PILBOP",
  "CBLARE",
  "RESARE",
  "DWRTPT",
  "ISTZNE",
  "PRCARE",
  "TSSCRS",
  "TSSLPT",
  "TSSRON",
  // The widening round: the deferred own-symbol classes.
  "ACHBRT",
  "BERTHS",
  "BRIDGE",
  "CHKPNT",
  "CONVYR",
  "CTSARE",
  "FERYRT",
  "FSHFAC",
  "FSHGRD",
  "HRBFAC",
  "LOCMAG",
  "LOGPON",
  "MAGVAR",
  "NEWOBJ",
  "PRDARE",
  // Its ORIENT-carrying features are directed legs and belong to
  // bin/generate-tss-anchors; only the undirected ones anchor here. See
  // ORIENT_LEG_CLASSES in bin/quilt-anchors.mjs.
  "RCTLPT",
  "RECTRC",
  "SMCFAC",
  "SWPARE",
  "WATTUR",
  "WEDKLP",
];

// The classes above whose area INTERIOR is painted -- by the vendored S-52
// lookup or by a Plotroom-owned client-side ground. These keep the original
// contract exactly: ONE anchor per (feature, interval), SCAMIN/SCAMAX
// inherited. The evidence, per class (packages/s52 data.json, area lookups of
// both PLAIN_BOUNDARIES and SYMBOLIZED_BOUNDARIES; both AC() colour fills and
// AP() pattern fills become MapLibre `fill` layers -- see
// packages/styles/src/instructions/SHOWAREA.ts):
//
//   RESARE   plotroom's restricted-area tint (frontend style.ts,
//            CHART_RESTRICTED_TINT_CLASSES) -- the vendored CS(RESARE04)
//            cascade itself draws symbols and lines only
//   CBLARE   the same plotroom tint list
//   SMCFAC   AC(CHBRN) in both boundary tables
//   TSSCRS   AP(TSSJCT02) in both boundary tables
//   TSSLPT   AP(TSSJCT02) under SYMBOLIZED_BOUNDARIES (the no-ORIENT row)
//   TSSRON   AP(TSSJCT02) under SYMBOLIZED_BOUNDARIES
//   PRCARE   AP(TSSJCT02) under SYMBOLIZED_BOUNDARIES
//   FSHFAC   AP(FSHHAV02) / AP(FSHFAC03/04) under PLAIN_BOUNDARIES
//
// NOT in this list, checked and worth recording: CTNARE -- its DIAMOND1 hatch
// was retired client-side (2026-08-06 user ruling, plotroom style.ts) and its
// lookups are SY+LS/LC only, so nothing paints a caution area's interior
// today; the perceived "magenta caution fill" is RESARE's tint.
const AREA_FILL_CLASSES = new Set([
  "RESARE",
  "CBLARE",
  "SMCFAC",
  "TSSCRS",
  "TSSLPT",
  "TSSRON",
  "PRCARE",
  "FSHFAC",
]);

// No-fill classes where a REPEATED symbol aids navigation (2026-08-21 user
// ruling, trimming the original all-no-fill list): the centred symbol (plus
// its text companion, e.g. SWPARE's "swept to X") is the only statement the
// area makes, and one point per elected component leaves it tens of km
// off-screen on a multi-cell swept area at high zoom. These classes emit a
// GRID of anchor points over the whole component -- roughly one per
// screenful at the deepest zoom the copy serves -- and drop SCAMIN/SCAMAX
// from the bag so the symbol serves the copy's whole interval (see the bag
// copy below).
//
// Written out POSITIVELY rather than derived: derived, a class added to the
// anchor set would silently take the riskier treatment (a repeated symbol
// and a stripped vendor SCAMIN) without anyone having looked at it. ADDING A
// CLASS TO AREA_ANCHOR_CLASSES MEANS ADDING IT TO EXACTLY ONE OF THIS LIST,
// AREA_FILL_CLASSES, OR SINGLE_ANCHOR_CLASSES -- the union test in
// test/generate-area-anchors.test.js fails until you do.
const GRID_REPEAT_CLASSES = new Set([
  "ACHBRT",
  "BERTHS",
  "CHKPNT",
  "CONVYR",
  "CTNARE",
  "CTSARE",
  "FSHGRD",
  "HRBFAC",
  "LOGPON",
  "PILBOP",
  "PRDARE",
  "SWPARE",
  "WATTUR",
  "WEDKLP",
]);

// No-fill classes the 2026-08-21 ruling REMOVED from the grid: back to the
// pre-grid contract in full -- one elected anchor per (component, interval),
// SCAMIN/SCAMAX inherited from the bag like every other class. (That keeps
// their old symptom too: a rescheme-era SCAMIN opens ~1.6 zooms after the
// copy's floor. Deliberate -- the ruling was a revert, not a half-revert.)
// BRIDGE polygons never fit a grid step anyway; RCTLPT/RECTRC directed legs
// belong to bin/generate-tss-anchors, and their undirected remainder plus
// the route/zone bands (DWRTPT, FERYRT, ISTZNE) and the magnetic pair
// (LOCMAG, MAGVAR) read better as one mark; NEWOBJ is a placeholder class.
const SINGLE_ANCHOR_CLASSES = new Set([
  "BRIDGE",
  "DWRTPT",
  "FERYRT",
  "ISTZNE",
  "LOCMAG",
  "MAGVAR",
  "NEWOBJ",
  "RCTLPT",
  "RECTRC",
]);

// ---- the grid --------------------------------------------------------------

// The tile grid the screen arithmetic is measured against. Matches
// bin/s57-to-tiles and the minzoom generators.
const TILE_PIXELS = 512;

// The top of the published pyramid -- bin/s57-to-tiles' publication_maxzoom.
// No step is ever sized past it: beyond 13 the client is overzooming the
// deepest tile it has, which STRETCHES the anchors already in that tile
// rather than asking for more of them, so sizing deeper would only pay in
// points at every zoom the pyramid does hold.
const PYRAMID_MAX_ZOOM = 13;

// Grid spacing ON SCREEN at the interval's sizing zoom (gridSizeZoom -- the
// deepest zoom the copy serves), in pixels of that pyramid: the 400-600 px
// "about one symbol per screenful" target. Sized at the DEEP end, so this is
// the interval's WORST case -- shallower in the same interval the same ground
// step spans fewer pixels and the symbols sit closer together, which is the
// safe direction. The ground step is this many pixels' worth of longitude at
// that zoom, with the latitude step scaled by cos(latitude) so the lattice is
// square in ground distance (and, equivalently, in Mercator screen pixels).
const GRID_SPACING_PIXELS = 512;

// Hard cap on anchors per (component, interval). A runaway component -- a
// swept band running the length of a coast at a deep sizing zoom -- would
// otherwise mint thousands of points; past the cap the lattice is COARSENED
// (step doubled, still a global lattice) rather than truncated, so coverage
// stays even instead of piling into the south-west corner. Logged when it
// happens.
const GRID_MAX_POINTS = 256;

// Iteration guard: lattice nodes per attempt, pre-coarsened from the bbox
// alone. Under the edge-driven scanline (latticeNodesContained) the node
// count is no longer the dominant cost -- the transient row buckets are,
// ~O(ring vertices) on real coastal geometry and O(vertices x rows) worst
// case for full-height edges (measured ~40 MB at 50k vertices x 68 rows) --
// but the cap stays: it bounds the emitted-node loop, the retry ladder, and
// that worst-case bucket memory all at once.
const GRID_MAX_LATTICE = 65536;

const args = process.argv.slice(2);

if (args.includes("--list-classes")) {
  process.stdout.write(AREA_ANCHOR_CLASSES.join("\n") + "\n");
  process.exit(0);
}

const inputs = [];
const evidencePaths = [];
const positional = [];
let dsnm = "";
let cellFloor = null;

for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
  if (args[i] === "--class" && args[i + 1]) {
    const value = args[++i];
    const separator = value.indexOf(":");
    inputs.push({
      className: value.slice(0, separator),
      path: value.slice(separator + 1),
    });
  } else if (args[i] === "--area-evidence" && args[i + 1])
    evidencePaths.push(args[++i]);
  else if (args[i] === "--dsnm" && args[i + 1]) dsnm = args[++i];
  else if (args[i] === "--cell-floor" && args[i + 1])
    cellFloor = Number(args[++i]);
  else positional.push(args[i]);
}

const outputPath = positional[0];
if (!outputPath || inputs.length === 0) {
  console.error(
    "Usage: generate-area-anchors <output.geojson> " +
      "--class <CLASS>:<file.geojson> [--class ...] " +
      "[--area-evidence <file.geojson>] [--dsnm <NAME>] [--cell-floor <N>]\n" +
      "       generate-area-anchors --list-classes",
  );
  process.exit(1);
}

for (const { className } of inputs) {
  if (!AREA_ANCHOR_CLASSES.includes(className)) {
    console.error(
      `generate-area-anchors: ${className} is not in AREA_ANCHOR_CLASSES -- ` +
        "widen the set there (and in bin/s57-to-tiles) deliberately, not by " +
        "invocation",
    );
    process.exit(1);
  }
}

// The cross-cell election, over the other cells' semantic roster; inert without
// one. See bin/quilt-election.mjs.
const election = anchorElection({ paths: evidencePaths, dsnm, cellFloor });

// ---- grid derivation (GRID_REPEAT_CLASSES only) ----------------------------

/**
 * The zoom the grid step is sized for: the DEEPEST zoom the copy serves,
 * capped at PYRAMID_MAX_ZOOM.
 *
 * The deep end, not the floor. A step fixed in GROUND units spans MORE screen
 * pixels the deeper the zoom -- degreesPerPixel = 360 / (TILE_PIXELS * 2^z),
 * so it halves with every level -- which puts the symbols FARTHEST apart on
 * screen at the interval's ceiling and closest at its floor. Sizing at the
 * ceiling makes GRID_SPACING_PIXELS the worst spacing anywhere in the
 * interval; sizing at the floor would make it the best, and every zoom above
 * it worse without limit. The three interval shapes:
 *
 *   bounded (`_QZMIN` + `_QZMAX`) -- the copy's own ceiling, capped.
 *
 *   open-ended top copy (`_QZMIN`, no `_QZMAX`) -- `_QZMIN + 2` is the
 *   representative deep zoom, capped. It is a choice and not a ceiling: past
 *   sizeZoom + 2 the on-screen spacing passes ~2048 px and keeps growing. But
 *   the pyramid stops at PYRAMID_MAX_ZOOM, so a top copy floored at or near
 *   it has no deeper TILE to be sparse in, and anything further is the client
 *   overzooming that same tile -- accepted sparse, rather than paid for with
 *   4x the points at every zoom the pyramid actually holds.
 *
 *   fallback (`_QFALL`, no `_QZMIN`) -- the open-ended band BELOW the cell's
 *   quilt floor, which carries no floor stamp of its own; the deepest zoom it
 *   serves is the quilt floor minus one, which IS the deep end and needs no
 *   cap (a quilt floor is never above the pyramid's top).
 *
 * A legacy input carrying no interval stamps at all (an unpartitioned cell,
 * which also gets no --cell-floor) returns null and keeps the one-anchor
 * behaviour: without a partition there is no interval to size a screenful
 * over, and what shipped before is what it keeps.
 */
function gridSizeZoom(range) {
  const min = Number(range._QZMIN);
  if (range._QZMIN !== undefined && Number.isFinite(min)) {
    const max = Number(range._QZMAX);
    if (range._QZMAX !== undefined && Number.isFinite(max))
      return Math.min(max, PYRAMID_MAX_ZOOM);
    return Math.min(min + 2, PYRAMID_MAX_ZOOM);
  }
  if (range._QFALL !== undefined && Number.isFinite(cellFloor))
    return cellFloor - 1;
  return null;
}

/** bbox-carrying entries over polygons, the shape polygonsContaining reads. */
function gridEntries(parts) {
  return parts.map((polygon) => {
    let minX = Infinity;
    let minY = Infinity;
    let maxX = -Infinity;
    let maxY = -Infinity;
    for (const [x, y] of polygon[0]) {
      if (x < minX) minX = x;
      if (x > maxX) maxX = x;
      if (y < minY) minY = y;
      if (y > maxY) maxY = y;
    }
    return { polygon, minX, minY, maxX, maxY };
  });
}

/**
 * The lattice nodes inside the component, spaced ~GRID_SPACING_PIXELS apart
 * on screen at `sizeZoom`.
 *
 * The lattice is GLOBAL: nodes sit at integer multiples of the step in
 * longitude and latitude, counted from (0, 0) -- never from this cell's bbox
 * corner -- so cells standing on one component derive the same node set from
 * the shared geometry whatever order the roster handed it over in. (Only the
 * elected emitter emits; the alignment is what makes that safe against
 * roster-order differences.) The step: GRID_SPACING_PIXELS' worth of
 * longitude at the sizing zoom on the TILE_PIXELS pyramid -- the same
 * degrees-per-pixel arithmetic as the minzoom generators' thinning cells --
 * with the latitude step scaled by cos(latitude) so the cells are square in
 * ground distance.
 *
 * How global, exactly: EXACTLY so in longitude -- the step is a pure function
 * of the sizing zoom, which every cell of one interval shares. In LATITUDE
 * the cos factor is taken not at the raw mid-latitude of the candidates'
 * joint bbox but at that latitude ROUNDED TO A 5-DEGREE BAND, because the
 * joint bbox is only identical across cells when the election's roster is
 * (the five classes never in the evidence roster -- BERTHS, CHKPNT, HRBFAC,
 * MAGVAR, RCTLPT -- and every ALONE or degraded group have an EMPTY
 * `verdict.parts`, leaving a per-cell bbox and a per-cell mid-latitude, which
 * drifted the latitude rows across cell seams: 47.0274 against 47.0564 for
 * the first rows of one measured component). Quantized, cells sharing a
 * component agree on the step unless their mid-latitudes fall either side of
 * a band edge -- rare, and cosmetic when it happens since cos changes by
 * under 8% across a 5-degree band at mid latitudes.
 *
 * Returns `{ points, coarsened, stepLon, stepLat }`; `coarsened` reports that
 * the step had to be doubled (kept a power-of-two multiple, still a global
 * lattice) to hold GRID_MAX_POINTS -- the caller logs it -- and the steps are
 * the ones the returned nodes actually stand on, coarsening included.
 */
function gridPoints(entries, sizeZoom) {
  let minX = Infinity;
  let minY = Infinity;
  let maxX = -Infinity;
  let maxY = -Infinity;
  for (const entry of entries) {
    if (entry.minX < minX) minX = entry.minX;
    if (entry.maxX > maxX) maxX = entry.maxX;
    if (entry.minY < minY) minY = entry.minY;
    if (entry.maxY > maxY) maxY = entry.maxY;
  }

  const degreesPerPixel = 360 / (TILE_PIXELS * Math.pow(2, sizeZoom));
  // Quantized to a 5-degree band so cells whose candidate sets differ still
  // agree on the latitude step -- see the note above. The same 0.01 floor
  // featureArea puts under cos, for the same polar guard.
  const latBand = Math.round((minY + maxY) / 2 / 5) * 5;
  const scale = Math.max(Math.cos((latBand * Math.PI) / 180), 0.01);
  let stepLon = GRID_SPACING_PIXELS * degreesPerPixel;
  let stepLat = stepLon * scale;

  // Nodes a step would test, from the bbox alone.
  const spanOf = (low, high, step) =>
    Math.max(0, Math.floor(high / step) - Math.ceil(low / step) + 1);

  let coarsened = false;
  while (
    spanOf(minX, maxX, stepLon) * spanOf(minY, maxY, stepLat) >
    GRID_MAX_LATTICE
  ) {
    stepLon *= 2;
    stepLat *= 2;
    coarsened = true;
  }

  for (;;) {
    const i0 = Math.ceil(minX / stepLon);
    const i1 = Math.floor(maxX / stepLon);
    const j0 = Math.ceil(minY / stepLat);
    const j1 = Math.floor(maxY / stepLat);
    // Edge-driven scanline, not a per-node polygonsContaining ray cast over
    // every ring vertex: a coastal band's rings run to ~10^5 vertices under a
    // bbox that pre-coarsens to the full GRID_MAX_LATTICE, and nodes x
    // vertices is minutes per (component, interval) -- what timed the
    // 2026-08 East Coast batch out. Same node set, same row-major order --
    // see latticeNodesContained for the equivalence, held bit for bit.
    const points = latticeNodesContained(entries, {
      i0,
      i1,
      j0,
      j1,
      stepLon,
      stepLat,
    });
    // < rather than <=: the guaranteed pointOnSurface anchor rides on top, and
    // the cap bounds the TOTAL per (component, interval).
    if (points.length < GRID_MAX_POINTS)
      return { points, coarsened, stepLon, stepLat };
    stepLon *= 2;
    stepLat *= 2;
    coarsened = true;
  }
}

// ---- 1. group every feature by (CLASS, LNAM, interval) ---------------------

const groups = new Map();
let anonymous = 0;

for (const { className, path } of inputs) {
  for (const feature of readFeatures(path)) {
    const properties = feature.properties ?? {};
    // A directed leg is bin/generate-tss-anchors' -- it stitches the parts back
    // into one lane and repeats an arrow along it, where this generator's
    // per-cell-copy grouping drew one arrow per part. Only the undirected
    // features of those classes keep their plain centred mark here. See
    // ORIENT_LEG_CLASSES.
    if (ORIENT_LEG_CLASSES.has(className) && hasOrient(properties)) continue;

    const parts = polygons(feature.geometry).filter(
      (polygon) => polygon[0] && polygon[0].length >= 4,
    );
    if (parts.length === 0) continue;

    const range = quiltRange(properties);
    // A feature with no LNAM has no identity to group on and is its own group
    // -- `#n` cannot collide with a real LNAM, which is hex.
    const lnam = properties.LNAM ?? `#${anonymous++}`;
    const interval = quiltKey(range);
    const id = `${className} ${lnam} ${interval}`;

    let group = groups.get(id);
    if (!group) {
      group = {
        className,
        range,
        // The rung of the copy ladder this group draws at, which the
        // election scopes its within-cell tie-break by: the copies of one
        // feature at different intervals cover disjoint zooms and all of them
        // emit.
        interval,
        area: 0,
        largest: null,
        // The identity the election compares across cells, and every part of
        // this cell's share of the feature to test adjacency with. Taken from
        // the FIRST fragment: the fragments of one LNAM are one feature and
        // carry one content, where `largest` is a placement decision.
        content: contentOf(properties),
        parts: [],
      };
      groups.set(id, group);
    }
    group.parts.push(...parts);
    group.area += featureArea(feature.geometry);
    // The largest FRAGMENT of the group -- outer-ring area, the same measure
    // bin/generate-labels ranks a multipolygon's parts by -- supplies both
    // the anchor point and the property bag.
    for (const polygon of parts) {
      const size = Math.abs(ringArea(polygon[0]));
      if (!group.largest || size > group.largest.size) {
        group.largest = { size, polygon, properties };
      }
    }
  }
}

// ---- 2. register every group with the election -----------------------------

// PASS 1, before any verdict is asked for. The election elects among ALL the
// groups standing on one component -- the several LNAMs this cell holds of one
// physical area as much as the other cells' parts of it -- so it cannot answer
// for any of them until it has been shown every one. See bin/quilt-election.mjs.
for (const [id, group] of groups) {
  election.register(
    id,
    group.className,
    group.content,
    group.parts,
    group.interval,
  );
}

// ---- 3. one anchor per elected component, in its largest fragment ----------

const features = [];
let elsewhere = 0;

for (const [id, group] of groups) {
  // The election: one anchor for the whole physical area, emitted by the cell
  // the component elected and, inside that cell, by the first of the groups
  // standing on it. A group alone in its component elects itself and nothing
  // changes.
  const verdict = election.verdict(id);
  if (!verdict.emit) {
    elsewhere++;
    continue;
  }

  // The placement polygon: the largest part of the WHOLE physical area -- this
  // cell's own clipped fragments and the elected component's polygons, ranked
  // together. The elected cell's own share can be a corner offcut a few
  // metres across, and a point inside it sits at the extreme edge of the area
  // (at overzoom visibly outside it). Strictly larger wins, so a tie stays with
  // this cell's own geometry.
  let placement = group.largest.polygon;
  let placementSize = group.largest.size;
  for (const polygon of verdict.parts) {
    const size = Math.abs(ringArea(polygon[0]));
    if (size > placementSize) {
      placementSize = size;
      placement = polygon;
    }
  }

  // The whole bag of THIS CELL's largest fragment, so the retargeted filters
  // branch exactly as they did on the polygon -- the bag is always this cell's,
  // even when the point above landed in a neighbour's part of the component: a
  // neighbour's attributes are another chart's compilation, not this one's.
  // Nulls are GDAL's unset columns and carry nothing; underscore properties are
  // the pipeline's own.
  //
  // SCAMIN/SCAMAX are the one REVERSAL, scoped to GRID_REPEAT_CLASSES: the
  // bag used to carry them for everyone, but for the no-fill classes the
  // partition's interval floor owns scale gating (the same position
  // bin/generate-area-edges takes for _AREA_EDGE), and rescheme-era NOAA
  // cells stamp SCAMIN at ~1.1x CSCALE, which the client gate
  // (z >= log2(279541132 / SCAMIN) on 512px tiles) opens ~1.6 zooms LATER
  // than the interval floor the copy serves -- a band where the copy is in
  // the tile and its only symbol is hidden. The FILLED classes keep
  // inheriting: their tint says the area is there whatever the symbol does,
  // and their vendor gating is left exactly as shipped.
  const stripScale = GRID_REPEAT_CLASSES.has(group.className);
  const bag = {};
  for (const [name, value] of Object.entries(group.largest.properties)) {
    if (name.startsWith("_")) continue;
    if (value === null || value === undefined) continue;
    if (stripScale && (name === "SCAMIN" || name === "SCAMAX")) continue;
    bag[name] = value;
  }

  // The GUARANTEED point, always first: the current single-anchor placement,
  // so a small component -- one the lattice below puts no node in -- still
  // gets exactly one symbol.
  const points = [pointOnSurface(placement)];

  // The grid, for the no-fill classes with a derivable interval floor. The
  // candidates are this cell's own clipped parts PLUS the component's roster
  // polygons -- the same union the placement above ranks, and deliberately
  // NOT restricted to this cell's own coverage: the single anchor has always
  // been allowed to sit in a neighbour's part of the component and renders
  // fine there, and the grid keeps that contract. (In the matched election
  // path the roster already holds this cell's own unclipped row, so the union
  // is the component's geometry for every participating cell; the own-parts
  // half only ADDS ground in the degraded no-own-row fallback, where this
  // cell is the sole emitter anyway.)
  const sizeZoom = GRID_REPEAT_CLASSES.has(group.className)
    ? gridSizeZoom(group.range)
    : null;
  if (sizeZoom !== null) {
    const entries = gridEntries([...group.parts, ...verdict.parts]);
    const {
      points: nodes,
      coarsened,
      stepLon,
      stepLat,
    } = gridPoints(entries, sizeZoom);
    if (coarsened) {
      console.error(
        `generate-area-anchors: grid coarsened to hold ` +
          `${GRID_MAX_POINTS}-point cap for ${id}`,
      );
    }
    for (const node of nodes) {
      // A lattice node NEAR the guaranteed point is the same symbol drawn
      // twice. The tolerance is a QUARTER of the step on each axis, not an
      // exact-coordinate epsilon: a node a metre away is not a second
      // statement about the area, it is one symbol over another, and at 1e-9
      // degrees (~0.1 mm) the test only ever caught the exact hit -- measured
      // over Puget, ~1.4% of gridded components carried a visible double.
      // A quarter step is ~128 px at the sizing zoom, comfortably wider than
      // any of these symbols and still far short of dropping a node that
      // reads as its own screenful.
      if (
        Math.abs(node[0] - points[0][0]) < stepLon * 0.25 &&
        Math.abs(node[1] - points[0][1]) < stepLat * 0.25
      )
        continue;
      points.push(node);
    }
  }

  for (const coordinates of points) {
    features.push({
      type: "Feature",
      // See ANCHOR_TIPPECANOE: the anchors ARE the symbol, exempt from
      // dot-dropping.
      tippecanoe: ANCHOR_TIPPECANOE,
      geometry: {
        type: "Point",
        coordinates,
      },
      properties: {
        ...bag,
        // Stamped AFTER the bag: the computed values win over any source
        // property of the same name.
        CLASS: group.className,
        // The whole component's area, not this cell's share of it: the style's
        // screen-size floor has to measure the FEATURE, and the elected cell's
        // share can be a corner offcut a few metres across. The verdict's area
        // is the component's polygons INCLUDING this cell's own unclipped row,
        // so it replaces the local sum rather than adding to it; the local sum
        // is what a group standing on no component keeps.
        AREA: verdict.parts.length > 0 ? verdict.area : group.area,
        // The interval the group's members serve, converted to tippecanoe
        // bounds later by bin/stamp-quilt-zooms.
        ...group.range,
      },
    });
  }
}

writeFileSync(
  outputPath,
  JSON.stringify({ type: "FeatureCollection", features }),
);
console.error(
  `Generated ${features.length} area anchors` +
    (elsewhere > 0 ? `, ${elsewhere} elected elsewhere` : ""),
);
